  <!-- Overview -->
  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The average person pays for 4–6 subscriptions they've forgotten about. Streaming services, SaaS tools, fitness apps, cloud storage — each auto-renews quietly, and the costs accumulate invisibly across different cards and billing cycles. SubScout is an iOS app concept designed to bring all of that into one clear view.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project focused on making complex, fragmented subscription data feel manageable: structured to reduce cognitive load, searchable and filterable for quick retrieval, and analytical enough to help users make informed decisions about what to keep and what to cancel.
    </p>
    <p>
      The work covered research, 18 user flow scenarios from core to edge cases, 28 screens of wireframes and high-fidelity designs, and a clickable Figma prototype.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Research -->
  <section class="section" id="research">
    <h2>Research</h2>
    <p>
      I began with research into how people currently track subscriptions — or fail to. Most rely on memory, bank statement scrolling, or informal notes. The problem isn't awareness that subscriptions exist; it's the absence of a single source of truth.
    </p>

    <h3>Core user scenarios identified</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The forgetter</strong> — pays for services they no longer use, discovers them only when checking a bank statement after the fact.</li>
      <li><strong>The optimiser</strong> — actively wants to reduce spend, but lacks a clear breakdown of what they pay, when, and whether it's worth it.</li>
      <li><strong>The sharer</strong> — splits subscriptions with family or flatmates and needs to track who owes what across shared plans.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Key pain points</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>No consolidated view — subscriptions live across different email accounts, app stores, and bank cards.</li>
      <li>Renewal surprises — upcoming payments aren't visible until they've already hit the account.</li>
      <li>Cancellation friction — users know they want to cancel but can't remember where or how to do it.</li>
      <li>No spend clarity — the monthly total is unknown until calculated manually.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      "I don't even know what I'm paying for until I check my bank app at the end of the month and see charges I didn't expect."
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- User Flows -->
  <section class="section" id="flows">
    <h2>User flows</h2>
    <p>
      I mapped 18 scenarios in total: 6 main flows covering the core product, 8 additional flows for secondary functionality, and 4 edge-case flows for error and empty states. This range ensured the design held up across realistic, irregular usage patterns — not just the happy path.
    </p>

    <h3>6 main flows</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Onboarding</strong> — first launch, permissions, and adding the first subscription</li>
      <li><strong>Add subscription</strong> — manual entry with name, amount, billing cycle, category, and renewal date</li>
      <li><strong>Dashboard overview</strong> — browsing all subscriptions with total monthly cost prominently shown</li>
      <li><strong>Search & filter</strong> — finding subscriptions by name, category, cost range, or billing frequency</li>
      <li><strong>Subscription detail</strong> — viewing full history, editing details, setting a cancellation reminder</li>
      <li><strong>Analytics view</strong> — monthly spend breakdown by category with trend over time</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>8 additional flows</h3>
    <p>
      Covering shared plan management, currency conversion for international subscriptions, notification preferences, archiving cancelled subscriptions, annual vs. monthly cost comparison, exporting a spend report, dark mode toggle, and widget configuration.
    </p>

    <h3>4 edge-case flows</h3>
    <p>
      Empty states for a new account with no subscriptions, error handling for invalid subscription data, interrupted onboarding recovery, and behaviour when a renewal reminder fires while the app is closed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Design -->
  <section class="section" id="design">
    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      28 screens across all flows. The design system prioritises data clarity above aesthetics — subscription data is inherently tabular and repetitive, so the challenge was making it scannable without feeling like a spreadsheet.
    </p>

    <h3>Information hierarchy</h3>
    <p>
      Each subscription row shows exactly what a user needs to act on it: service name and logo, monthly cost, next renewal date, and category tag. Secondary details — billing history, annual total, sharing status — sit one level deeper in the detail view. Nothing competes with the cost figure.
    </p>

    <h3>Colour and category system</h3>
    <p>
      Subscriptions are organised into categories — Entertainment, Productivity, Health, Storage, Other — each with a distinct colour token. The category colour runs as a subtle left border on each row and as a fill in the analytics pie chart, creating a visual link between the list and the breakdown without overloading the interface.
    </p>

    <h3>Upcoming payments</h3>
    <p>
      A dedicated section at the top of the dashboard surfaces renewals within the next 7 days. This directly addresses the most common pain point — renewal surprises — by making upcoming charges impossible to miss rather than buried in a calendar view.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Key Features -->
  <section class="section" id="features">
    <h2>Key features</h2>

    <h3>Subscription dashboard</h3>
    <p>
      The main view shows total monthly spend, an upcoming renewals strip, and a categorised list of all subscriptions. A persistent summary bar at the top keeps the total cost visible while scrolling through the full list.
    </p>

    <h3>Search and filtering</h3>
    <p>
      Full-text search across subscription names combined with multi-select filters: category, billing frequency (monthly / annual / weekly), cost range, and status (active / paused / cancelled). Filter state is preserved between sessions so frequent filters don't need to be reapplied.
    </p>

    <h3>Subscription setup</h3>
    <p>
      Adding a subscription takes under 30 seconds. A search-first entry field pulls from a library of 200+ common services with pre-filled logos and typical pricing, falling back to manual entry for anything unlisted. Billing cycle and renewal date are set in a single step using a native iOS date picker.
    </p>

    <h3>Analytics views</h3>
    <p>
      A monthly spend chart shows cost trend over the past 6 months. A category breakdown shows where money is going. An annual projection calculates the full-year cost including annual subscriptions — a figure most users have never seen before.
    </p>
  </section>

