  <!-- Reflections -->
  <section class="section" id="reflections">
    <h2>Reflections</h2>

    <h3>1 — Edge cases reveal structural weaknesses</h3>
    <p>
      Designing the 4 edge-case flows exposed gaps in the main flows that weren't visible during happy-path design. The interrupted onboarding recovery scenario, in particular, required rethinking how subscription state was persisted — a change that improved the main flow too.
    </p>

    <h3>2 — Data clarity requires restraint</h3>
    <p>
      The temptation with a data-heavy app is to show everything: full billing history, detailed analytics, granular filters, all at once. The design process was largely one of removal — hiding data that users don't need at that moment, surfacing it only when they navigate toward it.
    </p>

    <h3>3 — Next: real-data testing</h3>
    <p>
      The analytics views are based on assumed usage patterns. With real users and real subscription data, the most valuable chart or summary might be different from what was designed. A/B testing the dashboard layout against actual engagement would be the first post-launch experiment.
    </p>
  </section>

